    "narrative": "For LawDog on this September 11th slate, our offensive ceiling is anchored by **Cal Raleigh** (78.02), **Lawrence Butler** (72.57), and **Dylan Beavers** (69.52). **Cal Raleigh** benefits immensely from a soft matchup against a vulnerable arm, while **Lawrence Butler** is perfectly positioned to capitalize on a favorable park factor and a dynamic platoon advantage. **Dylan Beavers** is perhaps our most intriguing play; despite facing a veteran like Scherzer, the combination of his leadoff spot, positive park factors, and a slow-delivery opposing pitcher creates a perfect environment for him to exploit the stolen base category, which remains our primary focus in this 5x5 race. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** also earns a strong start (66.58), as his ability to leverage slow-delivery pitchers for stolen bases continues to provide elite utility value.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved the final outfield and utility spots, where **JJ Bleday** (45.11) and **Yainer Diaz** (41.14) just missed the cut. While **JJ Bleday** maintains a solid baseline, he was squeezed out by the efficiency of **Daulton Varsho** (45.29) and **Alex Freeland** (47.62). The optimizer ultimately favored the platoon-specific upside of **Daulton Varsho** against Rasmussen over **JJ Bleday**\u2019s profile. Meanwhile, **Yainer Diaz** was considered, but our commitment to **Cal Raleigh** and the necessity of maintaining game caps prioritized the higher-upside profiles of our starters.\n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the risk associated with **Bryce Harper** (49.40), who faces the elite stuff of Sale. While his name carries weight, the negative impact of facing such a high-caliber arm drops his projection into a marginal category, making him a \"start-only-if-necessary\" candidate. Furthermore, while we monitor for any late-breaking lineup changes for players like **Jonathan Aranda** (46.15) and **Masyn Winn** (51.13), the primary objective today is to balance our reliance on the speed profiles of **Dylan Beavers** and **Kyle Karros** (60.59)\u2014who also benefits from a slow-delivery pitcher\u2014to ensure we gain ground in the stolen base column without sacrificing our efficiency in batting average.",
